                        "entry_id": "725f35ee-e111-3226-9e6e-83e846bc1f72",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A cryptid in Inuit mythology and folklore in Canada and Greenland, a tall, dog-legged humanoid (sometimes identified with inland Native Americans)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3a5582f8-ed97-4910-8be7-089c75e928c7",
                                "sense_id": "0a556468-d9b6-3fcc-9b43-88cd62a1d913",
                                "text": "Adlets are wont to hunt on all fours, and so were born stories of giant black dogs that accost travelers."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "39b202a3-6f8b-4b3f-a5ec-16ce4ea13e0a",
                                "sense_id": "0a556468-d9b6-3fcc-9b43-88cd62a1d913",
                                "text": "An adlet is part-human and part-canine, […]. They can be cannibals, eating other dogs. When they first hunt prey they go for the victim's throat and drink the fresh blood."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "24471480-1d9c-4c98-98de-b5c0289c0168",
                                "sense_id": "0a556468-d9b6-3fcc-9b43-88cd62a1d913",
                                "text": "... that one of their ancestors has been married to a dog and that they have canine blood in their veins. This does not mean, of course, that they can transform themselves into an Adlet or that they enjoy any special protection from its attentions."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9b367a85-66d3-4045-a76c-a453337b4e50",
                                "sense_id": "0a556468-d9b6-3fcc-9b43-88cd62a1d913",
                                "text": "\"We could mate with humans, but then we'd have to expose our existence, and there's no guarantee that the baby will be an adlet.\" She cleared her throat. \"Of course, I'd love a human baby just as much as an adlet, but we need more halflings […]\"\n[…]\nThe dogs mated with other dogs and stayed in their canine form, but the halflings mated with both humans and dogs and so the adlets continued to spread across the land, always evolving. Some adlet families are more human than dog, ..."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
